Subject: KYRO AML – new version ready for your review

Hi [Name],

A new version of KYRO is ready, and I would value your review as our AML subject-matter expert before we take it further. The main changes are below. All data in this environment is synthetic.


1. Roles and access
-------------------
Access now follows the three-lines-of-defence model, with ten roles: Administrator, MLRO / AML Officer, Team Lead, Level 2 Investigator, Level 1 Analyst, KYC Analyst, QA Reviewer, Rules Owner, Auditor and Executive Viewer.

- Each role is defined by its responsibilities and its limits. For example, a Level 1 Analyst can clear an obvious false positive or escalate, but cannot close a true positive or recommend a SAR.
- SAR filing is reserved: only the MLRO (or an Administrator) can record a SAR.
- Four-eyes controls: nobody approves a rule change they proposed, or quality-checks a case they resolved.
- Auditor and Executive Viewer are read-only.

The roles in detail:

MLRO / AML Officer – the accountable AML officer: final say on suspicion and on the programme's rules.
  Responsible for: approving SAR filings; closing or returning escalated cases; approving rule changes and confirming obligations; assessing regulatory changes; assigning work and quality-checking cases.
  Limits: cannot approve a rule change they proposed; cannot manage users or system settings.

Team Lead – runs the alert queue day to day.
  Responsible for: assigning alerts; closing ordinary cases; escalating suspicion to the MLRO; completing KYC reviews.
  Limits: cannot approve a SAR or see SAR-filed cases; cannot change rules.

Level 2 Investigator – investigates escalated cases in depth.
  Responsible for: investigating and documenting findings; closing a case with no suspicion; recommending a SAR by escalating to the MLRO; uploading and reviewing documents; correcting customer details from documents.
  Limits: sees only cases assigned to them and the unassigned pool; cannot approve a SAR or see SAR-filed cases; cannot assign work or change rules.

Level 1 Analyst – first look at every alert.
  Responsible for: closing an obvious false positive, with a reason; escalating anything else.
  Limits: sees only cases assigned to them and the unassigned pool; cannot close a true positive or recommend a SAR; cannot see SAR-filed cases; cannot change customer records, documents or rules.

KYC Analyst – keeps customer due diligence current.
  Responsible for: completing periodic reviews and setting the rating after review; running screening and reviewing matches; collecting and checking customer documents; escalating concerns found during a review.
  Limits: sees only cases assigned to them and the unassigned pool, but every customer; cannot resolve transaction alerts; cannot see SAR-filed cases; cannot change rules.

QA Reviewer – checks that closed cases followed policy.
  Responsible for: sampling resolved cases and recording pass or fail with notes; reading the audit trail.
  Limits: cannot change a case's outcome; cannot check a case they resolved; cannot see SAR-filed cases.

Rules Owner – maintains detection rules and their link to regulation.
  Responsible for: proposing threshold changes with a backtest; extracting and confirming obligations; mapping obligations to controls; refreshing regulatory sources.
  Limits: cannot approve their own rule changes; cannot resolve cases; cannot see SAR-filed cases.

Auditor (read-only) – independent testing of the AML programme.
  Responsible for: reading everything, including the audit log.
  Limits: cannot change any record.

Executive Viewer (read-only) – senior management oversight.
  Responsible for: seeing dashboards and summary figures.
  Limits: cannot open cases, customers or documents; cannot change any record.


2. Who sees which records
-------------------------
- By assignment: analysts and investigators see only cases assigned to them and the unassigned pool. Team Leads, the MLRO, QA and Audit see all cases.
- By sensitivity: SAR-filed cases, their documents and the fact that a SAR was filed are visible only to the MLRO, Administrators and Audit.


3. Case detail view
-------------------
Each case is now a working file on one page:

- A stage tracker (Opened, Assigned, Investigation, Escalated, Decision, Quality check) and case age against a target.
- The rules that fired, the customer profile and risk rating, and how the customer's other alerts ended.
- Activity against the customer's own baseline (last 30 days versus the previous 90), counterparties and countries, accounts, and transactions with filters and export.
- Investigation notes and a full timeline, requests for information with due dates, documents, beneficial owners, and watchlist matches shown beside the customer with a recorded decision.


4. AI-generated case summary and draft narrative
------------------------------------------------
- A short summary is written for each case from the facts on file. A fuller draft narrative (who, what, when, where, why, what was done) is available for the analyst to edit.
- Customer and counterparty names are removed before anything is sent to the model, and every AI call is logged.
- The AI does not decide or recommend an outcome; the analyst does.


5. Regulations
--------------
- Regulation text (31 CFR Parts 1010 and 1023, 17 CFR 240.17a-8) is loaded with version history, and Federal Register notices are tracked.
- Obligations are extracted from the text for a reviewer to confirm, then mapped to the controls that meet them, with a traceability matrix.
- Rule threshold changes need a backtest and a second person's approval.


6. Data mapping
---------------
- A client's customer, account and transaction files can be loaded from CSV. KYRO suggests which column maps to which field, runs a check-only pass that reports errors row by row, and keeps a record of every load.


7. Other additions
------------------
- Quality-check workflow for resolved cases, with queue filters for cases awaiting or failing a check.
- Sanctions screening against the OFAC and UN lists, with whole-name matching to cut false matches.
- Document review: fields read from uploaded KYC documents and checked against the customer record.
- A work queue showing overdue cases and cases waiting on information, and a dashboard showing each user their own cases and, for leads, team workload.
- User administration and a searchable audit log.
- An exportable case report (PDF and CSV).


Where I would most like your view
---------------------------------
1. Do the role definitions and their limits match how an AML team should operate? Is anything missing or too permissive?
2. Is the SAR visibility restriction right, or should Team Leads also see SAR-filed cases?
3. Are the case stages and the information on the case page what an investigator needs?
4. Are the AI summary and narrative acceptable in wording and scope for a case file?
5. The case-age targets (5, 10 and 20 days by risk level) are placeholders. What should they be?


Known limits in this version
----------------------------
- Obligation extraction and rule mapping still use a simple keyword method, not the AI model, so most obligations are unconfirmed.
- The UK sanctions list is not loaded yet, and scanned documents cannot be read.
- Customer profile and account details are sample data.

I can walk you through it whenever suits you.

Regards,
[Your name]
